    unsigned int a = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // range is [1000, 9999]
    unsigned int b = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // range is [1000, 9999]
    unsigned int c = rand() % 9000 + 1000; // range is [1000, 9999]

    // Print the three integers
    printf("a: %u\n", a);
    printf("b: %u\n", b);
    printf("c: %u\n", c);

    // Add the three integers and print the result
    unsigned int sum = a + b + c;
    printf("sum: %u\n", sum);

    // Add the three integers using a long long and print the result
    unsigned long long sum2 = (unsigned long long)a + b + c;
    printf("sum2: %llu\n", sum2);

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that the output of this program is random and will vary each time it's run. The numbers printed in the `printf` statements are randomly generated, but they don't change within one run of the program. If you want them to be different each time, call `srand(time(NULL));` at the start of your main function (after `#include`) like this:

```c
int main() {
    srand((unsigned int)time(NULL));

    // rest of your code...
}
```
This will seed the random number generator with the current time, ensuring that each run gives different results.
']

