['Below is an example of Python code using Flask, a popular web application framework for Python. This script creates a very basic single-route web app with a regular expression check before the redirection. 

This simple web app has only one route `/redirect` that takes `target` parameter in query string and redirects to it if it matches the regex pattern, otherwise redirects to '/'.

```python
from flask import Flask, request, redirect
import re

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    return "Welcome to our website!"

@app.route('/redirect')
def redirection():
    target = request.args.get('target', '')  # get the target from query string, default ''

    pattern = re.compile(r'.*example\.com.*')  # regular expression that matches any URL containing example.com

    if pattern.match(target):  
        return redirect(target)
    else:
        return redirect('/')

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(debug=True)
```
To run the script, save it to a file (for example `app.py`), then in your terminal or command prompt, navigate to the directory where you saved the file and type `python app.py`. The web application will be available at http://localhost:5000/redirect?target=your_url
