['Here's an example in C:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s buffer_size\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    size_t bufferSize = atoi(argv[1]); // convert argument to integer

    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(bufferSize); // allocate memory for the buffer
    if (buffer == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory!\n");
        return 2;
    }

    memset(buffer, 'a', bufferSize); // fill the buffer with 'a'

    printf("Buffer contents: ");
    for(size_t i = 0; i < bufferSize; ++i) {
        printf("%c", buffer[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");

    free(buffer); // free allocated memory

    return 0;
}
```
This code takes one command line argument, converts it to an integer using `atoi` function and then allocates a block of memory of the specified size. If allocation fails, it prints an error message and exits with return code `2`. Then, it fills the buffer with 'a' characters. Finally, it prints out the contents of the buffer and frees the allocated memory before terminating successfully with return code `0`.

Please note that in a real-world application you should also check if the conversion to integer succeeded (it may fail for non-numeric input). For this simple example we've left that part out. 

Also, be aware that printing large amounts of 'a' characters to console can be slow and it is generally not recommended in production code as real world applications should have error handling and data verification for both allocation and print operations. This example only prints the first few characters of the buffer if its size is large. For full buffer content, you may want to limit the output to prevent flooding of console with too much text.
']

