#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024

void myFunction() {
    int i;

    // Allocate buffer
    char *buffer = (char*)malloc(BUFFER_SIZE);
    if (buffer == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory!\n");
        return;
    }

    for (i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        // Do some operations on buffer

        // Free the buffer every 5th iteration of loop
        if ((i+1) % 5 == 0) {
            free(buffer);
            printf("Freed memory\n");

            // If you want to use it again, allocate again
            buffer = (char*)malloc(BUFFER_SIZE);
            if (buffer == NULL) {
                printf("Failed to allocate memory!\n");
                return;
            }
        }
    }

    // Don't forget to free the buffer when you are done with it
    free(buffer);
}
```
This code first allocates a block of `BUFFER_SIZE` bytes, then enters into a loop which does some operation (which is not specified in your question so I skipped that part). After every 5th iteration, the memory allocated to the buffer is freed and again it gets allocated. In this way, the memory is freed after every use, but you are reusing the same pointer `buffer` for allocation. This might be useful if the size of data that needs to be allocated is not known at compile time or if we have multiple large buffers that need frequent allocation/deallocation and we want to avoid fragmentation.
Finally, don't forget to free the buffer after you are done with it as good practice (to prevent memory leaks).
']

