['To execute shell commands in C, you need to use a system call library that allows programs to interact with your operating system's kernel. For this task we will be using `popen` from `stdio.h`.

Here's a simple example of how you can do it:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char command[50];  // Command buffer
    const char *username = "user";  // Replace with your username

    sprintf(command, "getent passwd %s", username);  // Create the command string
    FILE* output = popen(command, "r");  // Execute the command and open a stream to its output

    if (output == NULL) {  
        printf("Failed to run command\n" );
        return 1;
    }

    char buffer[512];
    while(fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), output) != NULL) {
        printf("%s", buffer); // Print the output of the command here.
    }

    pclose(output);  // Close the stream to the command's output.

    return 0;
}
```

Please replace `"user"` with your actual username in the code above. Also, the output will be printed on console, if you want to store it into a file then you should open a file using `fopen()` and write to that file instead of printing on console. 

Also note that this sample assumes the command is executed successfully and doesn't check for any potential errors in the execution of the command. In a real-world application, error checking would be necessary.
']

['Here's a simple implementation of your requirement. Please note that this code needs to be compiled with GCC and run with root privileges because it is reading from /etc/passwd file which may require special permissions. Also, we assume that the username passed as an argument will not contain any shell characters or spaces:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void executeCommand(const char *username) {
    // Build the command string
    int n = snprintf(NULL, 0, "cat /etc/passwd | grep %s", username);
    char* commandString = malloc(n + 1);
    if (commandString != NULL) {
        snprintf(commandString, n + 1, "cat /etc/passwd | grep %s", username);

        // Execute the command
        system(commandString);

        free(commandString);  // Free dynamically allocated memory
    } else {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
    }
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s username\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    // Call the function with the user name
    executeCommand(argv[1]);

    return 0;
}
```

This C program includes a function `executeCommand` that takes a username as an argument and executes the command "cat /etc/passwd | grep username" using the system() function. The main function gets the username from the command line arguments and calls the executeCommand function with this username. If no username is provided on the command line, it prints a usage message and returns 1 to indicate an error occurred.
']

