['Here's an example of how you might implement this in C. This code assumes you have appropriate functions or libraries for handling file streams (e.g., `fopen`, `fwrite`), and that the escape_html function is already defined elsewhere in your program. 

Please note that error checking and memory management are not included here, so it's a basic example to give you an idea of how such a function might be structured. Also, this code assumes that there's a function `escape_html` which takes a string and returns the escaped version of that string.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Assuming escape_html is already defined elsewhere in your program
char* escape_html(const char* str); 

void http_hello_page(FILE* request, FILE* response) {
    const int MAX_USERNAME = 1024; // maximum length of username
    char username[MAX_USERNAME];

    if (fgets(username, MAX_USERNAME, request) != NULL) {
        // Remove trailing newline character if there is one
        size_t len = strlen(username);
        if (username[len-1] == '\n') {
            username[--len] = 0;
        }

        char* escaped_username = escape_html(username);

        fprintf(response, "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: text/html\r\n\r\n");
        fprintf(response, "<html><body>Hello %s</body></html>", escaped_username);

        free(escaped_username); // Don't forget to free memory allocated for escaped username
    } else {
        // Handle error: no input from request file stream
        fprintf(response, "HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request\r\nContent-Type: text/html\r\n\r\n");
        fprintf(response, "<html><body>No username provided</body></html>");
    }
}
```
This code reads the request file stream, which is assumed to contain a line with the user's name. The `http_hello_page` function then writes an HTTP response that includes the text "Hello [escaped username]". If no input is provided in the request file stream, it returns a 400 Bad Request error.
